      "text": "This all gathers to a point in Burke's church spire example, where we see not only that meaning is ascendant for Burke but that this valuation emerges from a world/human, nomos/physis dichotomy. Heidegger, however, is useful for understanding language without relying on or reinscribing such a binary. And so that we do not lose our way, note this is also the goal of an ambient rhetoric: to theorize rhetoric in ways that dissolve such problematic binary distinctions and bring the world and its processes back into what we mean by rhetoric. Yet I think we need to grant that in his own way, Burke too wrestled with this problem, even ifhe often slips back into an overemphasis on meaning that confounds his attempts to give the material and bodily their due. And certainly he has the rhetorical tradition overwhelmingly on his side, even if, as I attempt to show in my earlier discussions of the chora and kairos (see chaps. 1 and 2). there are other ways of understanding the relation of some of rhetoric's basic concepts to materiality. But once the human is defined as the \"symbol-using animal,\" it is nearly programmatic to assert that \"the logic of symbols must be 'prior' to the effects of any 'productive forces' in the socioeconomic meaning of the expression\" (Burke, RM 177). Still, we should treat this carefully, for it is tempting to read Burke as courting idealism here, although the presence of distancing quotation marks perhaps suggests some reservations on his part, some sense that there is material he has not yet quite adequately covered. If, however, symbolicity is prior to the generative effects of intuition, raw sense data, and immediate action, then the world shows up only through the nets of meaning. There is no unmediated access to the world. Riffing on Judith Butler, we might say that there is no symbolic action in the world that is not a further formation of that world (Bodies 10).5Thus a church spire is certainly a physical thing, but as Burke goes on to argue, \"we have once again come upon an area where nonverbal things, in their capacities as 'meanings; also take on the nature of words[;] ... the dialectical realm of ideas is seen to permeate the positive realm of concepts\" (RM 186). The ironizing quotation marks appear yet again, and more than that,\n\nii\n\nwe can see real consistency in the way Burke will play this deck of cards. Burke's world matters because it verbs, even if verbs matter because they are bound to bodies.\n\nBurke is wonderfully rich and forward thinking here. While we could wish for more precision and greater detail, he nevertheless intimates that to be a human being in the world necessarily involves intuiting that the world gives rise to meaning, since meaning emerges via the accretion of layers of abstraction over bodily emoting and gesture (Hawhee, Moving 109). Insofar as the world is meaningful, ideas and the tincture of persuasion they carry are inseparable from human affairs. But a few reservations or at least critical questions may be in order here. Thus I next look at some of Heidegger's work on these same issues-the questions of meaning, language, and language's relation to world-to clarify exactly how word relates to and evokes world and where we should then locate and describe the work ofrhetoric. I will argue for a further appreciation of the transformative effect oflanguage and its integration into the overt as well as tacit world of human meaning and activity. To get at such ideas, though, we must turn to Heidegger; I will begin with the earlier Heidegger of Being and Time, since he bears a greater affinity to Burke than the later Heidegger does.\n\n\"Oh, Baby, Baby, It's a Wild World\": Coping with Early Heidegger",
